WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.360
Segmentation separates systems so a mistake, compromise,

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:08.420
or overload in one area does not automatically affect everything else.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:14.000
A flat network allows too much unnecessary communication and increases blast radius.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:19.980
Segmentation introduces zones: user networks, server networks, management networks,

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:26.040
guest wireless, production, development, sensitive environments, cloud networks,

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.100
and third-party connection areas.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.320
Each zone should have a purpose and a reason for the communication it allows.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:37.500
A trust boundary is the place where communication should be controlled, inspected, logged,

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.640
or explicitly approved.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:45.540
Boundaries often sit at firewalls, access control lists, security groups, VPN gateways,

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:49.120
identity-aware proxies, or cloud routing controls.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:54.940
A boundary is useful when it reflects real risk: data sensitivity, business function,

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:00.840
administrative privilege, partner access, internet exposure, or operational criticality.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:05.380
It is less useful when it exists only as a diagram that no one operates.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.760
Segmentation should support the business.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:12.540
A payroll system, guest wireless network, building control network,

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:17.480
development environment, and production database do not need the same trust model.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.580
Some systems need tightly controlled access, while others need broad user reachability.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.680
Good segmentation creates clear communication paths and denies unnecessary paths.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.380
It should also support logging, troubleshooting,

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.000
and incident response so teams can understand what is happening across boundaries.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.900
Avoid designs that are so complex teams cannot maintain them.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.800
Overly complicated segmentation can lead to emergency exceptions,

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.380
undocumented firewall rules, and fragile troubleshooting.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.400
Start with critical zones, known data flows, and high-value administrative paths.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:54.260
Review the design as the environment changes.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.200
Effective segmentation is understandable, enforceable, and aligned with ownership,

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:01.740
monitoring, and change control.
